Checking that a change to the vibe-arm codegen doesn't change its output
========================================================================

A refactoring of the codegen should output the same assembly as before,
byte for byte.  The scripts here compile a corpus of programs to assembly
with two compilers, under each configuration of the codegen, and compare.

  gen.sh      compile the corpus with the compiler of a build tree
  compare.sh  compare two output directories of gen.sh
  rare.sml    a program for what the rest of the corpus never outputs

The corpus is regression/*.sml, examples/src/*, rare.sml and, if you have
it, the benchmarks of parallel-ml-bench (mpl/bench/*).  The configurations
are the two register allocators (-vibe-arm-regalloc graph, the default,
and local), -vibe-arm-symbols true, and each allocator with the target OS
faked as Linux.  The regression suite and the benchmarks are compiled
under both allocators, and every eighth of them under the others; the
examples and rare.sml under all five.

Nothing is assembled, linked or run: this checks that the output is the
same, not that it is right.  For that, there is bin/regression, with
-codegen vibe-arm.


Procedure
---------

You need a build tree (the build/ directory that "make" makes, with
bin/mpl and lib/mlton) of a compiler that has the vibe-arm codegen, and
the compiler itself (lib/mlton/mlton-mpl-compile) from before and after
the change.  "make -C mlton" builds only the compiler, as
mlton/mlton-mpl-compile, in a couple of minutes, and leaves build/ alone.

  # before the change (for example, with it stashed)
  make -C mlton
  cp -R build /tmp/base
  cp mlton/mlton-mpl-compile /tmp/base/lib/mlton/

  # after the change
  make -C mlton
  cp -R build /tmp/new
  cp mlton/mlton-mpl-compile /tmp/new/lib/mlton/

  cd mlton/codegen/vibe-arm-codegen/check
  ./gen.sh /tmp/base /tmp/out-base ~/parallel-ml-bench
  ./gen.sh /tmp/new /tmp/out-new ~/parallel-ml-bench
  ./compare.sh /tmp/out-base /tmp/out-new

compare.sh exits with status 0 if every assembly file is the same, and
names the files that differ if not.  One run of gen.sh is about 900
compilations (five minutes on a ten-core machine) and writes about
1 GB.  Set JOBS to change how many run at once, and ONLY to reg, ex,
pmb or rare to compile just that part of the corpus.


Notes
-----

- Use bash to run the scripts.

- The first line of every file that the compiler outputs is a comment
  with its version, which is different for a tree with uncommitted
  changes; compare.sh skips that line.

- gen.sh adds a target, lib/mlton/targets/fakelinux, to the build tree
  that it is given: a copy of the host's, with "linux" as its OS.  That
  is enough to make the codegen output what it does for Linux (symbols
  without a prefix, .L labels, :lo12: relocations, .hidden, unpacked
  stack arguments), but that output has not been assembled.

- A few programs don't compile, with either compiler: at the time of
  writing, four of the regression suite and three of the benchmarks.
  compare.sh requires only that the logs of the two runs are the same.

- rare.sml exists because the rest of the corpus never outputs indirect
  calls, overflow checks on unsigned and narrow words, conversions
  between reals and unsigned words, compare-and-swap on 16-bit values and
  on reals, or fnmsub.  It uses _prim and _import to get at them, and is
  only ever compiled, not linked.  When the codegen learns to output
  something new, check that some program of the corpus makes it do so
  (grep the output of gen.sh for the mnemonic), and add to rare.sml if
  none does.
